<PairedLayout
  title="First Action Fix — turning stalled signups into active accounts"
  description="Educational email for construction software: a fixed sequence that teaches the trial user in the order he meets the job, and reaches him at the moment of the decision."
  active="home"
>
  <section class="hero box">
    <h1>I help construction-software companies turn signups the product never activates into active accounts, using educational email.</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>For construction software with a trial or freemium tier — field management, bidding, scheduling, job costing — where signups arrive and most never reach a second session.</p>
    <p>Educational email is a fixed sequence that teaches one job the trial user already has, in the order he would meet it. A newsletter reports; this instructs, and ends inside the product.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>The order is the lever. Teaching lands before the demo, answers before the support ticket. A signup reads, applies, and comes back to an account already in use.</p>
    <p><a class="lateral" href="/versions/v2/educational/">the arithmetic on the visits that leave &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>The second lever is the moment. Calendar mail hits the week; a trigger hits the decision — the abandoned signup, the quiet trial, the upgrade page opened and closed. In the common case, 3–5 of every 100 trials pay with no mail at all; calendar drip carries that to about 5–7, and behavioral sends add roughly one more.</p>
    <p><a class="lateral" href="/versions/v2/behavioral/">where those points come from &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>Each lever is thin alone. Teaching that arrives on a schedule reaches a reader who has already moved on. A trigger that fires with nothing to teach is a reminder, and the inbox it lands in already takes around 120 messages a day from around 11 separate tools. Taught first, then reached at the moment — the sequence has something to say, and says it while the decision is open.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>It works where the product has an activation moment and a trial long enough to reach it. Behavioral sends work best where the events already exist and carry an identity, with roughly 90 days of history behind them; where they do not, drip is usually the better place to start.</p>
    <p>Under about 50 signups a month, a founder often does better writing personally. It will not fix a product nobody wants, or onboarding that breaks before the email matters.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>Email is one instrument among several that move the same numbers. The others — the billing plumbing, the friction preview, the review cadence — are laid out with what each is worth at a working scale.</p>
    <p><a class="lateral" href="/versions/v2/methods/">the six, and the arithmetic under each &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <section class="box">
    <p>The course is five days of lifecycle email, one a day, each usable on the current trial. The last asks what is still missing; the reply names what to build next.</p>
    <a class="act" href="#">Join the course &rarr;</a><!-- -> /course (external, other chat) -->
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p class="plain">The DM thread stays open.</p>
  </section>
</PairedLayout>
